  "verbalization": "Cabazitaxel is implicated in the inhibition of microtubules through a shared pathway with Ixabepilone and Eribulin, all of which bind to Tubb2a. The evidence suggests that Cabazitaxel, as part of the Microtubule Inhibition class, interacts with Tubb2a via a common mechanism of binding, although the exact nature of the interaction is not explicitly stated. The lowest common ancestors of Cabazitaxel and its related compounds point to Organic Molecular Entities, indicating a general class of compounds that may share a common biochemical pathway. The integration of these pathways implies that Tubb2a is a target of microtubule-inhibiting agents, and Cabazitaxel, through its inclusion in the Microtubule Inhibition class, may also act on Tubb2a via a similar mechanism. The evidence supports a relationship between Cabazitaxel and Tubb2a through the shared inhibition of microtubules, with Tubb2a being a downstream target of these agents.",
